we were discussing this design and wondered if it was a good idea to surface private stuff as the default view here, in case there is something dodgy in here and you have someone standing next to you :sweat_smile:
We felt that it would be better to only show private stuff if the user had explicitly clicked a "private" CTA.
@Gela Malek Pour suggested putting this private view behind biometric authentication as a partial mitigation. But we could also consider showing "regular" tabs first, as these tabs generally get more usage than private.

@Nicole Weber been thinking about the container colors over and over in my head. 

Do we go light on dark or dark on light? I know android often contains elements in darker containers, but also some key areas that are similar to this (e.g. notifications), do the opposite.

What do you think?

@Nicole Weber I wonder if we want to more closely match desktop colors for private browsing. 

I know a number of mobile browsers use this purple color for Private, but I personally don't really like it because it feels a little too colorful and breaks what I think is a cardinal rule for a browser ("get out of the way").

That said, if we go that route, we likely need some sort of other visual indicator that we're in private mode.

@Jeffrey Guerrero This step is a little abrupt to the HNT. I wonder do we want to include some language around "Last step..." and then we go to HNT the confirmation message? Something to make it more connected. 

And perhaps we make the transtion more subtle visually from the animation to the blurred version. Tagging @Rick Russie in case maybe he can help with this advanced animation?

cc @Eduardo Feo @Anthony Keane

@Anthony Keane @Brooke Katalinich - content rewrites by @Emily Wachowiak  - adding here for visibility

"I rewrote copy for 2 of the onboarding screens per UX management feedback today. I provided a few options for each one, and have placed a star next to the current favorites. Please let me know if you have any feedback!

Update: Meridel asked me to riff more on the "peace of mind" concept so I added a "POM" row with additional variations. I've starred two that I think are strongest."

@Rick Russie the menu could look something like this, the tools sections would work similar to the 'Display Options' section in Arc Search, just unfold; 

The priv/sec section should be smaller.

We probably don't need the header.

Shortcuts should be pin-able from home, so 'Add to shortcut' doesn't need to be super prominent here and can live in an overflow (that's missing here) or in the 'Edit' option at the bottom.

@Jeffrey Guerrero Check these options and let me know what do you think, I like the simple one with a small sync animation...

https://www.figma.com/design/N2oIr9aI3ynjjRgVaX733m/Illustration-Sync?node-id=0-1&t=iLhUJNquUVTBuCVn-1

Just some draft ideas.

@Jeffrey Guerrero 
I'm still unsure about this interaction since it primarily just displays the Sign Up button. Would it be better to streamline this step and directly prompt users to either create an account or sign in?

And of course the sign up/sign in flow is not necessarily a short step.

@Brooke Katalinich WDYT?

@Christopher Logan What do you think about adding a similar 'large/wide' design of search bar? Have you tried this out? 

It would have been weird/wrong if it were being used in the address bar/toolbar, but as we are placing it in the middle of the screen, I think we can try it out. 

This screenshot is from Chrome that uses 60dp search bar height.

@Jeffrey Guerrero I created another version of the confirmation card and placed it on the @Christopher Logan's HNT design. I've also added the a version of @Nicole Weber's toolbar to start to see how it is looking.  It is really coming together!

For the confirmation card, I do like the dark card UI with the blur and wonder if we just use the Fx logo and not the fox? Also, not sure we can use the close icon in the center like this but I added it for consideration.

cc @Eduardo Feo @Anthony Keane @Rick Russie

@Jeffrey Guerrero Animations - Design rational:
* Animated elements capture attention and make the onboarding experience feel more dynamic and interactive.
* Smooth transitions and microanimations prevent onboarding from feeling static or boring.

